-{"status":"needs-you"}
-{"icon":"toggle_on"}
-{"text":"Two hooks were armed to nothing. Both are now **one paste** — the exact JSON is written out, with what each turns on:\n\n**[settings-block.md](/framework/ai/2026-09-21/arm-the-hooks/settings-block.md)**\n\n**A `Bash` matcher** arms the reload hold's guard. Its `check()` half has always run on every write, but it can only fire if something recorded *who took a hold* — and a hold is taken by a plain Bash command, which no armed hook could see. It has been finding no record for any agent and correctly doing nothing since the day it was written. `ledger.mjs` now records that itself, so arming it is one matcher, not a new hook file.\n\n**A `UserPromptSubmit` array** arms the relay that rings me the moment you speak, instead of me finding out on my next timer. That's the one measurably fixable stage of the 29-minute median.\n\nI drove both with real hook payloads rather than reasoning about them — `hold.mjs on` wrote the record, `off` released it, and the ordinary write path was re-tested after and is unchanged.\n\n## What I left out on purpose\n\nA third idea — refusing `find /` from a pre-Bash hook — is **not** in the block. It would be a fourth thing on a list I'm trying to shorten, for a problem that has cost CPU but never data. Worth doing next time you're in that file anyway, not as its own ask.\n\nThese were two separate items on your list. They're one now."}
