-{"text":"The guard that blocked me was citing a 42-minute-old finding the file had already outlived. Friday it did the same with a 404 fixed 700 seconds earlier. Twice is a pattern, so it's fixed: it now compares the file's modification time against the finding's timestamp and skips evidence the file has outgrown.\n\nIt's an mtime check, not a re-fetch — a hook runs inside your write and mustn't do network I/O it could hang on. And if the newer write *also* broke something, the watcher logs a fresh finding, and that one still blocks. So this can only drop evidence that's genuinely out of date.\n\n## I nearly claimed a proof I didn't have\n\nMy first test was to write a file and observe that the guard no longer blocked. **That proves nothing** — that finding's key was already marked reported from the earlier block, so it could never have fired twice regardless of my change.\n\nCaught it before telling you. Tested the predicate in isolation instead, all three cases: a finding newer than the file still blocks; a file newer than the finding skips; a missing file falls back to blocking.\n\nThat's the third time today an absence nearly passed as evidence — the author filter, the empty `localStorage`, and now this. Same shape each time, and the fix is always the same: construct the state deliberately rather than inferring it from something not happening."}
