-{"text":"**Correcting what I told you half an hour ago.** I said V3 was broken for about eight minutes, 17:33 to 17:42. That figure isn't supported, and I withdraw it.\n\nI'd inferred it from one error plus the file's final save time. The health log actually holds **two** errors today, not one: `go_timeline is not defined` at 17:33:59 and `paint_view_tabs is not defined` at 17:41:33. Both name an atomic-write temp file, and none of those temp files remain.\n\nSo the file was being actively rewritten across that whole span — **two brief breaks during an edit sequence, not one continuous outage.**\n\n## I also over-claimed against myself\n\nI wrote that my probe at ~17:41 \"reported clean while the page was broken.\" With a second break at 17:41:33, a clean read just before it is perfectly consistent with the file being fine *between* the two. So my probe wasn't necessarily wrong.\n\nWhat stands: I asserted a clean bill from one flow through one view, which is a weak proof whatever the timing. What doesn't stand: that I demonstrably said \"verified\" during a break. Overstating my own error is the same failure as understating it — both put something in the record that the evidence doesn't carry.\n\n## One open question I'm not going to guess at\n\nBoth findings blame a temp file, which may mean the watcher is loading pages mid-write and catching half-applied edits. If so it can report breaks no visitor ever saw — the opposite of this morning's blind spot, and a false-positive source that would teach agents to discount it.\n\nI have no evidence either way. Guessing is what produced the eight-minute claim in the first place.\n\n**Verified before writing this:** both symbols resolve, `node --check` passes, four entry points render clean, no temp files left behind."}
