+I'm thinking in the AI2 dashboard on the the preview rail, we have an overview button that kind of links to a whole different view. And I, I'm trying to even remember what that looks like. Um, you know, I'm, I'm thinking we need like a prioritized view. And I think I asked for something like this. I don't know if we have it yet. I don't know if we can do cards of different sizes where like, you know, low importance things are smaller and big, you know, topics or big kind of like paging. It's really the class, the page class is like such a core concept that anything that's done regarding paging should probably be, you know, on the inbox should kind of appear as paging or class page or whatever. And, uh, and then have like sub notification that displays the actual event. But then it's just, it's a contextual log, right? Like the, it's not like the, anything necessarily happened to the page. Well, I mean, it might have, but the, the page itself just wraps the notification in the preview rail. I think we already have that and that works pretty well. Um, but that class page, you know, the, if there's a, a page, a card in the note of, in the, in the AI dashboard rail for the, the page class, I, what I was thinking is, is that we could render the actual page class right there in the inbox. And so the inbox is sort of just a different navigation mechanism for like recent things. And so I, I don't think that I don't find any problem rendering the page class at a s separate URL. It would be like, you know, the AI2 dashboard slash. I don't know. I don't know if we, we have a system for rendering any URL inside the. Uh, AI inbox, but that's actually kind of the way that I'm, I'm thinking it could work rather than have to create a new uh, task or card or whatever on the file system, which is, I guess, in the short term isn't a problem if that's easier. But being able to, you know, if, if we're doing work on the page class, creating a task like card for the page class that's supposed to resemble work on that page, but we can't even see that page. It's like if we could just link to the actual page and put the uh, stuff in the right place, like the notifications in the right place and the updates or the suggestions or the decisions or the notifications or activity log or whatever, whatever's going on on that page that's the reason for its you know presence in the inbox rail, um, you know, it just... We, sh we show it kind of in in its real context. And so then any updates we're making there, you know, it's the exact same page as the framework core page. It's just, like I said, a different way to jump there. And then the, the cool part there is that then the, the inbox rail, which is time-based, right? The newest things come to the top. If, if we then are doing some work on Servex, boom, class Servex pops to the top of the list and I can click over to it and see exactly what it looks like. Though that's the familiar view for each of the things that I'd like, you know, rather than creating a secondary view that's just overly complicates things and you know, you got to learn a secondary view. Let's just use the main views that we got. And, you know, the, the detail area is like a full... height and can be a fill width type area and so which you know the the class doc pages are you know pretty decent i mean they're not terrible at filling the width they can go full width as well and so i think that works uh, i think that makes sense and then the the sidebar rail should obviously be contextual to you know the drawer should be contextual to whichever page is actually active and frankly it should be the same AI sessions as if I were on the actual page. Um, yeah, I think that makes sense.
+
+## Continued (about 3:10 PM)
+
+Yeah, so for the inbox uh, rail thing that I'm talking about, um, if there's an existing page, then we shouldn't need to make a new page. We should be able to put tasks on class doc pages. You know, we, we should be able to put anything anywhere, frankly. And so I don't know, I don't know the best way to do that yet because, uh, you know, if we, let's say we create a new module or, or UI or whatever, like some new widget, and we want to use that widget on different pages in different places, we might want to create a registered content module system so that those classes can be registered with the app and then any page that's loading dynamic content from a JSON file, for example, can then use, you know, just by specifying the type and the class name, can use content modules from any kind of defined uh, system. That does create a little bit of a dependency uh, trap. Um, but I think that's, that feels like the kind of the right, right picture. Um, The alternative is is using JS files, which I think we might have a system for having kind of like arbitrary JS chunks where we can actually import things. So maybe that's a better alternative because then the then you get the proper error if you've imported something that doesn't exist or or whatnot, and so it's less uh, mysterious. But yeah, so the idea there is that you know if we can put anything anywhere, like it. I, I don't want to say it doesn't matter where the data is stored. In fact, putting tasks into the class. folders themselves actually makes kind of a lot of sense. Um, so may, I think that is probably a decent move rather than having everything in this AI dashboard. Uh, you know, we put things in their proper location. You know, yeah, I think that makes sense. Um, now, I, I think we want to keep the, the actual code directories. We don't want to be right now. The AI dashboard is very much bloated. It's chaos. It's, you know, so many sessions and masterminds and loose ends and, and not getting shit done. We want to do the opposite for the uh, actual framework pages. And so we want to be more careful about creating uh, proper tasks and structures and organizing them in the right place and well, and making sure they get done and they don't go stale or unfinished and that they get done properly and, and whatnot. Um, but yeah, the, the point about you said inbox rows are the real pages card. And it's like, I think what you're meaning is that the, the preview loads up in a, in a page from somewhere else. Cause that's kind of what I was, I was explaining. And that's true when, that's possible. And I think we want to lean into that almost as much as possible. So if there's, there should rarely, as, as infrequently as possible, we create some random new card in the dashboard. However, we do want to have temporary cards. Like if I want to say just, you know, create a new card in the dashboard, boom, you create a new card and then, you know, we can start working inside of it. Um, and that would, you know, if there's not a better place for it to live, that would just live right there in the And the dashboard and then in terms of how the AI2 dashboard actually kind of uh, surfaces these references to other pages it you actually would need some system to um, you know logging system essentially to put to bring it to the top of the list to you know draw the preview of the card to then draw the um, you know the full page You know, render the full page there, and so like there would need and and then if it uh, if that page is re-rendered, like you know, we obviously can cache it or at least the way that our pages show and hide right now, like we wouldn't need to re-render it. Um, yeah, so ho hopefully, I, I think that should all work pretty pretty easily.
