+## Your words (09-29, about 1:45 PM)
+
+All right, so I've tried to design a layout explorer in the past, and it hasn't really worked. We have a lot of pages with layout previews, page previews, you know. But here's what I'm thinking is the way it needs to work. We need a left preview rail that activates the main, you know, it turns a preview into like a full view in the center. And then in the right sidebar, by default, we show alternates. And so through this process, it's sort of like a, a tree with, uh, you know, the main preview area in the center as like a full, it doesn't necessarily have to be full zoom. It could be like a responsive zoom or some one of these responsive viewports we have. I'm not sure what where all those are at, but the idea is, is that as you're browsing this tree of kind of, The main left sidebar should be the most primitive primary, you know, like there, there, there could be different ways to browse layouts and, and really they're pages, right? I think, you know, each layout should be encapsulated in its own page, at least, you know, when we're documenting these things. And so um, the, the left sidebar is, you know, probably start with like one column layouts, two column layouts. Um, maybe we have... like some scale based uh, categories like mobile layouts versus mega layouts. But anyway, you select one of these categories on the side from the preview rail, and then you see the most basic version of it in the main area. and, you know, we can work on, you know, what that looks like. But the then the, the right sidebar is sort of like the child items within that. And so you get to preview the one that you're clicking on in like a larger mode. And then when you click on a child one, then the basically the way I, I'm envisioning this is that the what used to be the right sidebar becomes the left sidebar. So all the siblings and then the currently selected one becomes the main view as the large center full size preview. And then any sub you know items from there go into the right sidebar. So the right sidebar is always kind of like the children previews. And you know these should be little preview cards. Like each page could have a little preview card. Now for for a lot of these layouts, the page preview may, maybe instead of using a, the page preview, we just use the full view, but we render it at a scaled down preview card that's you know like linkable and clickable. So um, this three column layout explorer, we should be able to get every single layout in the entire uh, site documented in this layout tree. And, you know, click through each one and, you know, in terms of the, the right sidebar drawer that has like properties in it. Um, okay, what I want you to do is I want you to spawn a minion or a mastermind or whatever, create a task to over do an overview of the, the EXT drawer process. I think recently I was asking about, you know, taking over the left sidebar, the framework sidebar. What we really want to do is do a, a, a study of the HTML structures for our paging systems. So and this gets kind of complicated because with the column sidebars, in order to have column nested column pages where each page has its own pages container and have the columns all be equal width, the way that the that you know you figured out how to do that in the past was to use display contents and so the nesting the higher the HTML hierarchy and the layout hierarchy have diverged and are kind of two different there's and, and I think it's there's different modes I think column pages do that to display contents but I think normally tab pages and other pages are just you know using a pages container and, and using active classes to swap themselves out however when it comes to the The, the thing that I really want to you know look into with this drawer kind of review is what kind of HTML structure do we have for like the home page down to the framework page down to the you know at all levels of this uh, kind of website we're rendering different templates or shells or sidebars or you know we have lots of different layouts and uh, It, it, it seems a little broken. I think we might have a few extra containers or something. Um, some of it might be optimal, but maybe we just need to tweak, you know, uh, some CSS or something. I'm not actually sure what's going on there. Um, but when it comes to having potentially multiple sidebars at multiple levels, um, you know, with, with the EXT drawers, I don't know if we have like named sidebars like app.com. Right or app dot left or or what you know we would want to name them but like having reusable uh, I mean I was asking I think yesterday about whether sidebars should be reusable or whether we sh you know and then they would have like a paging system within them to swap out you know when we render something into a column one of those sidebars and then we want to swap it out to some you know when we click on something else to select something else I'm not really sure how that works But also then like, well, what if we wanted to have multiple left sidebars or right sidebars? I'm not sure if we really want to, but we might want to. And how those are stored, I, I would imagine that we would want a handle, a good reference to each one in the app structure or in the page structure. So the page, any, well, the app could have a left and right sidebar drawer. It, well, either a persistent left or right sidebar, like a navigation. That may or may not collapse and then could also have a drawer that you know opens kind of above that as like a shell and a page could have a, a left sidebar drawer that opens you know within that and pushes things over I don't know there's a lot of different ways to to set that up but we definitely want to work on uh work on that system especially in this three column layout explorer because I'm not sure if we want the dev so we have the dev bar we have the right sidebar for you know like selecting It seems like it's uh, available now on, I don't know if it's on every page, but for selecting items within a page and, and, and talking about them. By the way, that seemed to be broken. Uh, I, I was able to select on, when I opened up the hamburger menu on the AI2 dashboard page, I was able to select an element and, um, and then I, I tried to do a dictation you know, to ask about that element I had selected and it said it didn't see the selected element. So it, it did appear in the UI. I selected a code block and it, it had a little label that said this code block and it had a little X on it, a little pill that was kind of that contextual UI that uh, a lot of AI systems have. Um, and so that seemed to work, but then when I sent the message off, the assistant, at least the fast assistant said, I don't see that. Context, so somehow the context isn't getting to, you know, pump, pumped through to the LLM as part of the prompt. 
